<div class="meta">Vocab 0020 · pairs with lesson 0020</div>
<h1>Vocab: Socket Contracts</h1>
<dl>
<dt>socket</dt>
<dd>A named attach point a part declares in its own local space
(<code>head_round.sockets["eyes"]</code>). Children snap to it; the child
never knows which parent it landed on.</dd>

<dt>anchor</dt>
<dd>The implicit origin every part attaches BY — here bottom-center of the
part's bounding size, matching the game's feet-on-ground position
convention.</dd>

<dt>per-pair offset table</dt>
<dd>The anti-pattern: attachment positions keyed by (parent, child) pairs.
Grows quadratically, and every missing pair is a visual bug.</dd>

<dt>category spec</dt>
<dd>The row that wires a category into the assembly: which parent category
it attaches to and via which socket name. The assembly is a fold over these
rows, parents before children.</dd>

<dt>style tag</dt>
<dd>A compatibility family on each part ("round", "blocky", "any"). One
predicate gates the validator, picker, and randomizer so incompatible art
styles never mix.</dd>

<dt>assembly space</dt>
<dd>The unscaled coordinate frame parts are placed in before the single
size-contract scale. Socket math happens here; world sizing happens once,
afterwards.</dd>

<dt>record independence</dt>
<dd>Persona and look live in separate tables/structs joined only by
character_id at the spawn site — either can change, migrate, or fail
without touching the other.</dd>
</dl>
